The strongest consensus across these styles is: start with enough easy volume to build durability, keep one or two controlled quality sessions, and only add race-specific work once the body can absorb it. Peak Stride’s version is especially good for runners who get injured easily or need a clear “build first, sharpen later” framework, while elite-documentary channels are better for seeing what very fit runners actually do in a real block. The best practical plan is usually a hybrid: mostly easy running, one threshold-oriented workout, one speed-maintenance element like strides or hills, and periodic adjustments based on recovery and the target race.

A solid template
A sensible weekly template, based on the overlap of these approaches, would be:

2–4 easy runs for aerobic volume.

1 threshold session, kept controlled.

1 strides or hill session to preserve speed and mechanics.

1 long run.

1–2 rest or cross-training days if needed.

A short taper before racing, with reduced volume but some speed kept in the legs.